    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d573">

                <pb facs="0876" n="774" sameAs="frus:frus1933v01/pg774"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">550.S1 Monetary Commission/58</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Chief of the Treaty
                        Division</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Barnes</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-01-17">January 17, 1934</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Barnes</hi>: I took this matter up with the
                    President yesterday and asked him to initial his O. K., if he approved, opposite
                    the second paragraph of Senator Pittman’s letter. Inadvertently he initialed at
                    the top of the letter,<anchor n="16" xml:id="d573-f1"/> but you may take his O.
                    K. as indicating that the proclamation constitutes ratification.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">William Phillips</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d573-f1" n="16" xml:id="d573-f1-content">
                <p>The notation was as follows: “This is O. K. FDR Jan 16” (550. S1 Monetary
                    Commission/57).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
